# Answer 8 · The Intersectionality of Gender with Other Social Identities (CSS 2025)
@py question 2025-6

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define intersectionality simply, trace its origin, then analyse gender's overlap with each identity — with Pakistani evidence — and close with policy and critique.
@ask Give a DETAILED ANALYSIS of the INTERSECTIONALITY of GENDER
@ask with OTHER SOCIAL IDENTITIES
(1) Introduction: identities overlap and multiply disadvantage; Crenshaw 1989; ask "which women?"
(2) Origins: Sojourner Truth 1851; Combahee 1977; the DeGraffenreid case; Crenshaw's traffic image
(3) Key ideas: interaction not addition; matrix of domination; privilege and disadvantage together
(4) Gender and class: poor women's work, transport and access to justice
(5) Gender and place: rural women's distance from schools and clinics
(6) Gender and religion or sect: minority women's added risks
(7) Gender and ethnicity or caste: Hazara, Baloch and scheduled-caste women
(8) Gender and disability, age and gender identity: disabled women, widows, girls, khawaja siras
(9) Why it matters: better data, better laws, better targeting
(10) Criticisms: endless categories; hard to measure; charged as divisive
(11) Conclusion: equality for "women" must begin with the women at the overlaps
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Give a detailed analysis"** asks you to **take the concept apart**: what it means, where it came from, how it works, and what it shows. **"With other social identities"** tells you to go through **several identities** — class, place, religion, ethnicity, disability, age, sexuality and gender identity — and show how each **interacts** with gender. Pakistani evidence under each identity turns a textbook answer into an analysis.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Intersectionality** is the idea that a person's social identities — **gender, class, race, religion, ethnicity, caste, disability, age and sexuality** — **overlap and interact**, producing **unique experiences** of privilege and disadvantage that cannot be understood one identity at a time.

#### ii) Origin of the term
The American law professor **Kimberlé Crenshaw** coined it in ==1989==. It grew from discussions on **gender and race**, but now covers every identity.

#### iii) The argument
Intersectionality is not a new school so much as a **tool**: before saying anything about "women", it asks **"which women?"** In a country as diverse as Pakistan, gender policy that ignores this question reaches the women who need it least.

### 2. Origins

#### i) Early voices
**Sojourner Truth**'s speech "**Ain't I a woman?**" (Akron, Ohio, ==1851==) pointed out that the ideal of the protected "lady" never applied to enslaved Black women. The **Combahee River Collective** (==1977==) described **interlocking** oppressions experienced **simultaneously**.

#### ii) Crenshaw's case
In ***DeGraffenreid v. General Motors*** (==1976==), Black women laid off by GM sued for discrimination. The court said GM hired women (white women) and hired Black people (Black men), and refused to combine the claims. Black women fell **between** the categories of race and sex.

#### iii) The traffic image
Crenshaw compared discrimination to traffic at an **intersection**: a woman standing where two roads cross can be hit from either direction — or both.

### 3. Key ideas

#### i) Interaction, not addition
Disadvantages do not simply **add up**; they **multiply and change** each other. A poor woman does not face "sexism plus poverty" but a **different situation**: she cannot afford private transport, so she rides crowded buses where she is harassed.

#### ii) The matrix of domination
**Patricia Hill Collins** (*Black Feminist Thought*, ==1990==) described a **matrix of domination** — intersecting oppressions organised through **structures, institutions, culture and personal life**.

#### iii) Privilege and disadvantage together
The same person can be **privileged** in one identity and **disadvantaged** in another. An educated, upper-class Pakistani woman faces sexism, but she may employ a domestic worker who has no labour protection.

::: fig venn | Intersectionality: overlapping identities create a unique position.
@centre Poor, rural, minority woman: multiplied disadvantage
Gender: a woman in a patriarchal society
Class and place: poor and rural
Religion or ethnicity: a minority
:::

### 4. Gender and class

#### i) Work
Female labour-force participation in Pakistan is ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25), but the reasons differ by class. Poor women work, often **unpaid** or as **home-based piece workers**; middle-class women are often kept out of work by norms of **respectability** and unsafe transport.

#### ii) Justice
A poor woman facing violence cannot afford a lawyer, a phone or a bus to the court. Laws exist for all; **access** depends on class.

### 5. Gender and place (urban and rural)

#### i) Education and health
Female literacy is about ==53%== nationally (Census ==2023==), but far lower among **rural** women. Rural women live farther from schools, clinics and courts; the maternal mortality ratio of ==186== per 100,000 live births (PMMS 2019) hides a sharper rural burden.

#### ii) Climate
In the ==2022== floods, rural women in Sindh and Balochistan faced loss of livelihood, lack of privacy in camps and risks in pregnancy — gender, place and poverty together.

### 6. Gender and religion or sect

#### i) Minority women
**Hindu and Christian** women and girls face added risks, including reported cases of **forced conversion and marriage**, especially in Sindh and Punjab. **Ahmadi** women face both gender norms and legal and social discrimination against their community.

#### ii) Within the majority
Sect and interpretation also shape women's lives — for example, in access to mosques, inheritance practice and dress codes.

### 7. Gender and ethnicity or caste

#### i) Ethnic minorities
**Hazara** women of Quetta have lived with targeted attacks on their community; many are widows carrying both trauma and the burden of family survival.

#### ii) Caste
**Scheduled-caste** Hindu women in Sindh face poverty, bonded labour and caste prejudice alongside gender norms.

### 8. Gender and disability, age and gender identity

#### i) Disability
Women with disabilities face barriers of **access**, **stigma** and **marriage prospects** that neither disabled men nor other women face in the same way.

#### ii) Age
**Girls** face early marriage — Islamabad set a minimum age of ==18== in ==2025== — while **widows and elderly women** often lose property and support.

#### iii) Gender identity
***Khawaja siras*** stand at the overlap of gender identity, poverty and exclusion; the **Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2018** and its court challenges show how contested their position remains.

::: table | How gender interacts with other identities in Pakistan.
@widths 3.2,10.8
Identity | What the overlap produces
Class | Unpaid and home-based work; no access to lawyers or safe transport
Place | Distance from schools, clinics and courts; climate risk
Religion | Reported forced conversions; community discrimination
Ethnicity or caste | Targeted violence; bonded labour; prejudice
Disability | Inaccessible services; stigma; marriage barriers
Age | Child marriage; widows' loss of property
Gender identity | Exclusion from family, work and inheritance
:::

### 9. Why intersectionality matters

#### i) Better data
Averages hide the worst-off; data must be **broken down** by sex, place, class, disability and religion.

#### ii) Better laws and policy
Programmes must be designed **for the women at the overlaps** — for example, mobile health teams for rural women, legal aid for poor women, and protections for minority girls. The **Benazir Income Support Programme**, which pays poor women directly, is an example of targeting at the overlap of **gender and class**.

#### iii) A better movement
It keeps feminism from speaking only for the **urban middle class** — a criticism often made of Pakistan's women's movement.

### 10. Criticisms

#### i) Endless categories
Critics say the list of identities can grow forever, **fragmenting** the movement.

#### ii) Hard to measure
Multiplied disadvantage is harder to count than single categories.

#### iii) Divisive?
Some argue it divides people by identity; supporters reply that it **reveals** divisions that already exist. Crenshaw herself described it as a **lens** for seeing how power works, not a ranking of who suffers most.

### 11. Conclusion
Intersectionality shows that **"women" is not one group**. Gender interacts with class, place, religion, ethnicity, disability, age and gender identity to produce very different lives. For Pakistan — ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026 — the lesson is practical: equality for women must **begin with the women at the overlaps**, or it will reach only those who need it least.

## Why this answer scores
- The concept is **defined simply**, with its **origin, case and image** — the examiner sees understanding.
- Each identity has its **own heading** with **Pakistani evidence** — a true "detailed analysis".
- A **Venn figure** and a **table** show the overlaps at a glance.
- **Why it matters** and **criticisms** give balance, and the conclusion turns theory into policy.
